<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karlovac</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karlovac is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, at the meeting of the Kupa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korana">Korana</a>, Mrežnica and Dobra rivers. It was founded in 1579 by Archduke Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as a star-shaped fortress of the Military Frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and named after him. It later grew into a trading town on the routes from the Pannonian plain to the Adriatic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karlovac.jpg" alt="Karlovac" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bukovacka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karlova%C4%8Dka_zvijezda-_Izvor-_Digitalni_Tisak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karlovac lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, 56 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and 130 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a>. Four rivers give it the name grad na četiri rijeke, the town on four rivers: the Kupa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korana">Korana</a> and Mrežnica flow through the built-up area, and the Dobra a few kilometres outside the centre. Croatians also call it grad parkova, the city of parks. Most of the parks are planted in the former trenches dug around the old fort, and one part of the centre keeps the name Šanac, trench.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Austrians built Karlovac from scratch in 1579 to strengthen their southern defences against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. It was part of a bargain between the Protestant nobility of Inner Austria and Archduke Charles II: in return for religious freedom the nobles agreed to pay for a new fortress. It was a six-pointed star on the Zrinski estate near the old town of Dubovac, begun on 13 July 1579 under the architect Matija Gambon. It was built on terrain exposed to flooding and unhealthy water, deliberately, to hamper the Turkish advance. A fire in 1594 burned all its buildings, and they were rebuilt by 1610.</p><p>As an outpost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, Karlovac was one of the first headquarters of the general command of the Military Frontier. The Uskok leader Ivan Vlatković, whose raids and piracy troubled the Habsburgs, was executed there on 3 July 1612. Ottoman forces besieged the city seven times, the last in 1672, and never took it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699 it lost its military importance and became a market for wheat, corn, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and tobacco.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> in 1766. A plague in 1773 killed almost half the population. In 1776 she restored the city to the Croatian crownland, and in 1781 Joseph II confirmed it as a free royal town. It flourished on its roads to the sea and its river traffic until the railway of 1861 ended its era as a major trade centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Karlovac is the administrative centre of Karlovac County; its population was 49,377 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The star shape of the old fortress can still be seen around the centre. The city suffered damage in the Croatian War of Independence of 1991 to 1995, and a former Austrian barracks at Turanj now holds a museum of its military history. Its music school, founded on 1 December 1804, is one of the oldest in this part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karlovac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 16th century Ottoman conquests had reduced Croatia to a narrow band around Zagreb, <b>Karlovac</b> and the coast, the reliquiae reliquiarum of the old kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Karlovac</b> was founded in 1579 as a frontier fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/military-frontier">Military Frontier</a></li></ul></section>
